{"ts": "2026-11-05T00:40:36.047396Z", "run_id": "r-20261105T0040Z-f642", "unit": "openspec:add-interpreter-core", "seq": 7, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-002", "target": "F-a71db6eb", "decision": "reject", "by": "agent", "reason": "The finding holds as a test gap, not a code defect. src/interpreter/runtimes/block.ts calls EffectGate.before inside runStep(nodeId) and EffectGate.after inside runStep(`${nodeId}#done`), and moving after into the node step fails two tests (checked by mutation on the unit head). But the scenario \"Gate order, decisions and failures\" (spec.md:40) pins the step of each gate call: the first case logs exactly `pay: before(CALL, CTX)`, `pay: invoke(CALL, CTX + idemKey ik_1)` and `pay#done: after(CALL, CTX + idemKey ik_1, { output: PAID })`. In block-effect.test.ts the rig tracks the running step in `current`, but no logging gate or invoker reads it, so a block that called after inside the pay step and then ran an empty pay#done step would pass every test. The step that holds each gate call decides what a replay runs again, and U34's effect journal relies on it, so the tagging must be tested as the scenario writes it. Only the test changes: the logging gate and the logging invoker prefix each entry with the step that is running. The Q-003 reject already makes a fix unit, so this adds one test task to it. WHEN the block runtime runs node pay with an effectful tool, a gate answering { action: \"invoke\", idemKey: \"ik_1\" } and an invoker answering PAID, on WorkflowOps that record the running step THEN the gate and invoker calls are logged with that step, exactly `pay: before(CALL, CTX)`, `pay: invoke(CALL, CTX + idemKey ik_1)` and `pay#done: after(CALL, CTX + idemKey ik_1, { output: PAID })`, and executed is [\"pay\", \"pay#done\"].", "text_sha256": "829cddd5bcacac21bafe87177bfc56db0f97f5536bf6c825d4b9516887116e10", "answer_sha256": "2153b474411fc8d775b07f8726064eee88effe0e160589d003df4dfda5475152", "marker": "CLAUDECODE"}
{"ts": "2026-11-05T00:40:36.048927Z", "run_id": "r-20261105T0040Z-f642", "unit": "openspec:add-interpreter-core", "seq": 8, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-003", "target": "F-257bab9b", "decision": "reject", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding is correct against the design. Design Decision 9 says a thrown value is an interpreter error when its code is in InterpreterErrorCode, not by instanceof, because a workflow engine may rethrow a recorded step error as a copy whose shape is inferred and unchecked. The predicate shared by visit.ts and the block runtime requires error instanceof Error in addition to a recognised code, so a code-bearing copy that is not an Error instance falls to the fallback and becomes INTERPRETER_TOOL_FAILED with the fixed message, losing its code, node and details in the failed row and the run error. The repository does not state the shape of DBOS's replayed step error, which is exactly why the design chose a code-only rule. WHEN a step of a node runtime rethrows a value whose code is an InterpreterErrorCode but which is not an instance of Error, as a replayed copy of a recorded step error may be THEN the visit and the block runtime keep that code, node and details in the failed row and the run error instead of mapping the value to INTERPRETER_TOOL_FAILED.", "text_sha256": "f277ddda01ff8f017810ea819ea0b371695696eb5bcc8ce64de861c4045ca9e1", "answer_sha256": "362cb4b2282873e9048bbb00a03b512267d346ce04dc2edf8b1d79249f9d7f3e", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
{"ts": "2026-11-05T00:40:36.050015Z", "run_id": "r-20261105T0040Z-f642", "unit": "openspec:add-interpreter-core", "seq": 9, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-004", "target": "F-8da8966d", "decision": "accept", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding is waived: the judge saw only the part of the diff without visit.ts, and the task's text is done at the unit head. The failure test file exists with tests for both the node-failure and run-level scenarios. execute.ts holds the revisit guard that fails a twice-visited node with INTERPRETER_LOOP_LIMIT, the terminal-run shortcut that returns a terminal run after run:load, and the run-level failure mapping for a missing invoker and unloadable versions. visit.ts, the file the judge did not see, holds the node failure mapping that writes a failed row with the RunError and rethrows a fixed InterpreterError, passes durable cancellations and store write errors through unchanged, and never copies a raw tool error. Ticking 3.3 as already satisfied by the 3.2 implementation is therefore consistent with the code.", "text_sha256": "3986f14b440d3f20241e8e9de26a9361297160a9bb8bba795c194d3e14190476", "answer_sha256": "9617d6796bb5461515901d4466a8bb8ac0ed2ce657efb95b1ad87f8741dad4f5", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
